{"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Listening"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Listen to the audio. Move the words from the box to correctly complete the paragraph. <br/>You have 2 attempts to submit your answers."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le02ob05re5aud01"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"type": "template_override", "content": 2}], "id": 12}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n3le02ob05re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Charlie: </span> I came across this brochure about OOP. There was a bunch of these displayed in the main hall of the faculty. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Mark: </span> What does it say? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Charlie: </span> It says that the big difference between OOP and procedure programming languages is that instead of writing a long piece of code, people who program with OOP decided to divide long ago information in objects, which they defined as encapsulated data. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Mark: </span> I know that there are two key concepts for understanding OOP, which are objects and classes. The brochure must portray this. </br>"}, {"time": "36000", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob05re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Charlie: </span> Indeed. For stating the difference between those concepts, the authors use the example of a washing machine. They define class as a general category in which some other elements can be contained. For instance, \u201cmachines\u201d would be the class for the washing machine. Now, what does the class define? It refers to two sets of features: properties and methods. Properties are the variables or data and methods, the functions. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Mark: </span> Let me guess. When talking about the washing machine, properties must be all the features related to height, weight, price and brand. Regarding methods, we can talk about concrete actions such as washing, rinsing and drying. </br>"}, {"time": "84000", "image": "enpe8n3le02ob05re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Charlie: </span>  Exactly. According to the brochure, a programmer made all the description as an abstract idea, a blueprint of something through certain class in OOP, the object is the concrete idea, the representation of the data and a program execution. The brochure reminds us that designers built each object from a class and each class is designed and programmed to accomplish various tasks. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Mark: </span>  What's that information at the end of the brochure about? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Charlie: </span>  It says that many apps, such as Textfield and Array, are using this OOP.  </br>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n3le02ob05re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "title": ""}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}